    ('b8eb874b-6cf0-422b-aa69-59cc2753dd30', 'appenzeller-spitzhauben', 'Appenzeller Spitzhauben', 'Appenzeller Spitzhauben', true, 'Layers', 'Appenzeller Spitzhauben chickens originated in Switzerland and were developed during centuries ago. They were developed in the Appenzell region as a hardy free-ranging farm chicken. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

Appenzeller Spitzhauben chickens have a forward-pointing crest, V-shaped comb, and compact mottled body. FlockFront classifies this profile as Layers. Hens lay large white eggs, typically 150–200 eggs per year. They are generally flighty. They are agile, cold-tolerant foragers that need secure fencing and overhead protection. Egg output varies with age, season, nutrition, lighting, health, and management, so the production figure is a practical breed range rather than a guarantee.', 'layer', 'white', '150_200', 'Flighty', true),
