    ('ab0be360-7bae-46fb-b767-d1fa33ff2bbb', 'sultan', 'Sultan', 'Sultan', true, 'Layers', 'Sultan chickens originated in Turkey and were developed during nineteenth century. They were imported from the Ottoman Empire to Britain as an ornamental palace fowl. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

Sultan chickens have a crest, beard, V-comb, feathered legs, five toes, and abundant white plumage. FlockFront classifies this profile as Layers. Hens lay large white eggs, typically 150–200 eggs per year. They are generally calm. They do best on dry ground with crest and foot-feather care and protection from assertive flockmates. Egg output varies with age, season, nutrition, lighting, health, and management, so the production figure is a practical breed range rather than a guarantee.', 'layer', 'white', '150_200', 'Calm', true),
    ('c30ad2f1-c245-498e-8848-0c7d5b786d6f', 'sumatra', 'Sumatra', 'Sumatra', true, 'Layers', 'Sumatra chickens originated in Indonesia and were developed during nineteenth century. They were derived from long-tailed island fowl imported from Sumatra and neighboring islands for exhibition. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

Sumatra chickens have lustrous dark plumage, a long flowing tail, pea comb, and multiple spurs in males. FlockFront classifies this profile as Layers. Hens lay small white eggs, typically under 150 eggs per year. They are generally active. They are strong fliers and agile foragers that need secure spacious housing. Egg output varies with age, season, nutrition, lighting, health, and management, so the production figure is a practical breed range rather than a guarantee.', 'layer', 'white', 'under_150', 'Active', true),
    ('c42ba137-4cbf-407a-8e32-756839e2fe80', 'sussex-buff', 'Sussex - Buff', 'Sussex - Buff', true, 'Layers', 'Sussex chickens originated in England and were developed during ancient roots; standardized in the nineteenth century. They were developed from regional farm chickens of Sussex and neighboring counties for dependable eggs and table use. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

Sussex chickens have a broad rectangular body, single comb, and substantial frame. FlockFront classifies this profile as Layers. Hens lay large brown eggs, typically 200–250 eggs per year. They are generally friendly. They forage well and adapt to varied climates; large combs need attention during severe cold. Egg output varies with age, season, nutrition, lighting, health, and management, so the production figure is a practical breed range rather than a guarantee.', 'layer', 'brown', '200_250', 'Friendly', true),
    ('6ccb05e7-404e-4bea-9229-dcc991bb7582', 'sussex-light', 'Sussex - Light', 'Sussex - Light', true, 'Layers', 'Sussex chickens originated in England and were developed during ancient roots; standardized in the nineteenth century. They were developed from regional farm chickens of Sussex and neighboring counties for dependable eggs and table use. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

