    ('b419baa8-a049-496b-96eb-6ce0a6f83492', 'wyandotte-black', 'Wyandotte - Black', 'Wyandotte - Black', true, 'Layers', 'Wyandotte chickens originated in United States and were developed during late nineteenth century. They were created by American breeders as a rose-combed utility chicken, with Silver Laced the first established variety. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

Wyandotte chickens have a broad rounded body, rose comb, and dense feathering. FlockFront classifies this profile as Layers. Hens lay large brown eggs, typically 200–250 eggs per year. They are generally calm. They tolerate cold well, adapt to range or confinement, and may become broody. Egg output varies with age, season, nutrition, lighting, health, and management, so the production figure is a practical breed range rather than a guarantee.', 'layer', 'brown', '200_250', 'Calm', true),
