    ('ebe52fc7-4e57-4e19-afa0-d607c92d0c15', 'araucana-black', 'Araucana - Black', 'Layers', 'Araucana chickens originated in Chile and the United States and were developed during twentieth century. They were developed from Chilean blue-egg-laying stock and standardized in the United States as a rumpless, ear-tufted breed. Their history reflects the practical and cultural priorities of the farmers and breeders who maintained them before modern poultry production narrowed the number of commonly used breeds.

Araucana chickens have a rumpless body, pea comb, and distinctive ear tufts. FlockFront classifies this profile as Layers. Hens lay medium blue eggs, typically 150–200 eggs per year. They are generally alert. Breeding ear-tufted birds requires care because the tuft gene can reduce hatchability when inherited from both parents. Egg output varies with age, season, nutrition, lighting, health, and management, so the production figure is a practical breed range rather than a guarantee.', 'layer', 'blue', '150_200', 'Alert', 2360),
